                        IMPLEMENTATION OF MULTIPLE DIMENSION
                            CONTAINER ARRAYS IN PASCALINE

OVERVIEW

Pascaline requires both that multiple dimension arrays can be used with general,
or container, arrays. It also requires that container arrays be able to be
passed as value parameters. This brief gives an overview of the method used to
implement them.

SIMPLE GENERAL ARRAYS

The single dimension general array specification uses the concept of a "tagged
pointers" for its implementation. This means a pointer of the format:

A -> Base address of array
B -> Length of array

These pointers allow dynamic allocation of container arrays, as well as passage
of var or view parameters by passing a tagged pointer to the array.

COMPLEX GENERAL ARRAYS

For container arrays of more than one dimension, we introduce a new pointer that
can index an array of any number of dimensions, but still occupies only two
registers. To do this, the second parameter of the tagged pointer is a pointer
to a template describing the array with all of its dimensions, instead of the
length.

The template is a series of length words, one for each of the dimensions in the
target array:

x[a, b, c, ... n]

1. length a.
2. length b.
3. length c.
...
N. length n.

So a complex tagged pointer is represented as:

A -> Base address of array
B -> Pointer to template

A complex pointer is "progressive", that is, when an array indexing operation is
completed for one index level, the template pointer is advanced to the next 
length in the template. So, in the above example the different array indecies
are:

x[a, b, c, ... n]

A -> Base address of x[a, b, c, ... n]
B -> length a.

A -> Base address of x[b, c, ... n]
B -> length b.

A -> Base address of x[c, ... n]
B -> length c.

Etc.

The case of a simple tagged pointer to a simple array is kept in the new scheme.
Thus, when the next last index is calculated, and the next array is simple, the
next length is actually picked up from the template, and the pointer changes
from a complex to a simple tagged pointer.

TYPES OF ARRAY POINTERS

The result of this is that there are actually three formats for an array 
pointer:

1. Fixed dimension.
2. Simple container.
3. Complex container.

All of these formats are used, and all can and will be converted from and to
each other as part of normal processing.

CREATING TEMPLATES

Templates need to be both created from scratch, and also passed to allow 
existing fixed arrays to be passed as container array pointers. For each dynamic
container reference, the template is part of the tagged pointer allocation.
The template is filled out when the container array is created.

The second kind of template occurs when a fixed array is passed via a container.
In this case, the template is created as part of the static constants in the
code, since it represents constant dimensions in a fixed array.

CREATING, USING AND DISPOSING OF DYNAMIC CONTAINER VARIABLES

Any number of dimensions can now be represented by a dynamic pointer variable.
For the code:

program test;

type a = array of array of integer;

var r: ^a;
    b: integer;
	c: integer;

begin

   new(r, 10, 10);
   r^[b, c] := 1;
   dispose(r)

end.

In intermediate form:

!   new(r, 10, 10);

lodadr     
limint     
limint     
newgar     

!   r^[b, c] := 1;

lodadr     
ldimgp     
lodadr     
ldiint     
arfmar     
cvtmtg     
lodadr     
ldiint     
arfgar     
limint     
stiint     

!   dispose(r)

lodadr     
ldimgp     
dspgar     

Each action will be considered. First, the format of r is:

1. The base address of the space for the array.
2. The major index length.
3. The minor index length.

The allocation of the array is done by pushing the two length specifications
onto the stack and executing "newgar". This extends the existing intermediate
code. The action of the newgar intermediate is to multiply the indecies, find
the total allocation, get the space, plant the base address, and fill out the
template with the index values. Obviously the code generated differs from the
original meaning of newgar.

The reference to starts as normal with a lodadr to r. Then, instead of a lditgp
normally used to load the array tagged pointer, the ldimgp intermediate is used.
Instead of loading an address/length pair, it loads the base address, then skips
that address to index the first word of the template.

After picking up the indexor (b), the next operation would normally be arfgar,
or calculate array reference with a tagged pointer. Instead, the complex 
operator arfmar is used. It calculates the index operation using the template,
then replaces not only the base address with the calculated address, but also
advances the template pointer to the next location.

The interesting thing about arfmar is that, in order to find the true offset of
the indicated element, it must know the lengths across the entire template. 
Thus, it actually reads forward to examine the entire template, but only 
advances the template pointer by one.

Next, since this is a 2d array, the next index will be the last, and the complex
tagged pointer can now be converted to a simple tagged pointer. THis is done via
cvtmtg, which simply loads the length from the template pointer to replace the
pointer.

The last index calculation is then done with the familar arfgar operator.

The dispose operation is done via the standard dspgar operator. The pointer is
loaded with ldimgp, but it makes no real difference, since dspgar only looks at
the base address of the space to be disposed.

VAR AND VIEW PARAMETER PASS MODES

The var and view parameter pass modes are very little changed from simple to
complex modes. Since the complex tagged pointer occupies the same space as a
simple pointer, the allocations for it do not change, only the way the pointer
is constructed during the calling sequence, and the method by which it is used
internally.

program test;

type a = array of array of integer;

var q: array 10, 10 of integer;

procedure x(var z: a);

var b, c: integer;

begin

   z[b, c] := 1

end;

begin

   x(q)

end.

Intermediate:

! z[b, c] := 1

lodadr 
ldimgp 
lodadr 
ldiint 
arfmar 
cvtmtg 
lodadr 
ldiint 
arfgar 
limint 
stiint 

!   x(q)
prcbgn     
lodadr     
cvtftm     
prccal     

The access to the 2d array is almost identical to the first example. The pointer
is loaded via the var parameter as a complex by ldimgp, then the first index
calculation occurs via arfmar. The complex pointer is converted to simple for
the last index calculation by cvtmtg, then the last row calculation occurs using
the standard arfgar instruction.

For the call sequence, the address of the fixed array is loaded, then converted
to a complex pointer by cvtftm. This instruction loads the B side of the tagged
pointer to index a template in the constants area that is created by the
compiler to describe the existing fixed array. The compiler only need do that
for fixed arrays that are actually passed as complex arrays, although this does
create the possibility that multiple modules may each have such a template. This
occurs because a global array would have a template needed for each referencer.

The example would be identical for a view parameter, except that assignments to
the array would be forbidden by the nature of the parameter.

VALUE MODE PARAMETERS FOR CONTAINERS

New to Pascaline is the ability to pass both simple and complex containers via
the value mode. The way this is done is to simply create a copy of the 
parameter, then pass the address of that copy. Thus, the procedure or function
using the parameter actually treats this identically to a var or view parameter,
which were also handled identically under previous rules.

The exact location of the copy is left undefined. What is passed as a parameter
must be fixed length, and is the double word tagged pointer (complex or simple).
The copy data cannot go directly in line to the stack, since that would destroy
the fixed sense of the stack parameter frame.

The copy could go to heap storage, a special side stack, or other arrangement.
However, the full production encoder can easily look ahead to the allocated
parameters, and place them on the stack before the parameter frame. Thus it can
perform the copy allocation on the same stack, and reduce the runtime costs.

program test;

type a = array of array of integer;

var q: array 10, 10 of integer;

procedure x(z: a);

var b, c: integer;

begin

   z[b, c] := 1

end;

begin

   x(q)

end.

Note that this is the same program as above, but with a value mode parameter.

Intermediate:

! z[b, c] := 1

lodadr 
ldimgp 
lodadr 
ldiint 
arfmar 
cvtmtg 
lodadr 
ldiint 
arfgar 
limint 
stiint 

!   x(q)
prcbgn     
lodadr     
cvtftm     
cpymgp
prccal     

The code is almost identical, the difference is the insertion of the cpymgp
instruction. This instruction takes the complex pointer and copies it to an
"unspecified" location, then replaces the complex pointer to the real location
with a complex pointer to the copy. Note that this does not require a new
template. The template pointer that was used for the real parameter is simply
used to form the new complex pointer, and only the base address of the array
changes.

The code within the procedure itself it unchanged, and it is the same as the var
parameter case. The parameter is modified, but the modifications occur to the
copy, which is discarded.

The code for the simple container example is similar:

program test;

type a = array of integer;

var q: array 10 of integer;

procedure x(z: a);

var b: integer;

begin

   z[b] := 1

end;

begin

   x(q)

end.
 
Intermediate:

!    z[b] := 1

lodadr
lditgp
lodadr
ldiint
arfgar
limint
stiint

!    x(q)

prcbgn
lodadr
cvtftg
cpytgp
prccal

The code in the procedure is identical to the old method using var parameters.
The address of the parameter is loaded, and lditgp used to load the simple
tagged pointer. The array calculation takes place with arfgar.

The caller code loads the address of the actual array, then converts that to a
tagged pointer with cvtftg. The new instruction, cpytgp, does the same thing as
cvtmtg, but for a simple tagged pointer. The array is copied to an undefined
location, then the base pointer is replaced. The length stays the same.

The code to process the case of loading an existing complex array (not a 
reference created to a fixed array) is:

program test;

type a = array of array of integer;

var q: ^a;

procedure x(z: a);

var b, c: integer;

begin

   z[b, c] := 1

end;

begin

   x(q^)

end.

Intermediate:

! z[b, c] := 1

lodadr 
ldimgp 
lodadr 
ldiint 
arfmar 
cvtmtg 
lodadr 
ldiint 
arfgar 
limint 
stiint 

!   x(q)
prcbgn     
lodadr     
ldimgp
cpymgp
prccal

The only difference here between this and the first complex example is the use
of ldimgp instead of cvtftm. ldimgp loads the base addres pointed to by the
address, then sets the length template pointer to that address incremented past
the base address.

The simple container example is also trivially different, and is not shown here.     

